# unit-501 post-hoc — which lines move, and on which stream

## 1. One plant vs two: identical stdout LENGTH, different stdout BYTES

d1 stdout 3935B · d3 stdout 3935B
line counts 38 vs 38

differing stdout lines: 3
  L2
    one plant : "  tracked files read: 1052"
    two plants: "  tracked files read: 1053"
    byte lengths: 26 vs 26
  L3
    one plant : "  population (compares against both braces): 66"
    two plants: "  population (compares against both braces): 67"
    byte lengths: 47 vs 47
  L6
    one plant : "      UNGUARDED  59"
    two plants: "      UNGUARDED  60"
    byte lengths: 19 vs 19

every moved line keeps its byte length: true
  -> the 3935 figure survives a change in the number of reported defects ONLY because every
     figure that moved kept its digit count. This is the same coincidence that has protected
     4160 across four units, and it is the first time it hides a CONTENT change rather than
     merely surviving one.

## 2. Green vs red on stdout: which lines the red state DROPS

green 41 lines · red 38 lines
differing lines between green and red stdout: 6
  L2
    green: "  tracked files read: 1051"
    red  : "  tracked files read: 1052"
  L3
    green: "  population (compares against both braces): 65"
    red  : "  population (compares against both braces): 66"
  L6
    green: "      UNGUARDED  58"
    red  : "      UNGUARDED  59"
  L40
    green: "DECL-BODY SLICERS OK — no new hand-rolled slicer, no stale baseline entry."
    red  : undefined
  L41
    green: "  NOT CHECKED: whether the baselined files produce wrong FIGURES. This grades the reader each file uses, not the numbers it has already published."
    red  : undefined
  L42
    green: ""
    red  : undefined

## 3. The red stderr in full, for the record — never captured by anyone before 11-22

one plant : 722B · 8 lines
two plants: 795B · 9 lines

```

RED — 1 new hand-rolled brace-balance body slicer(s):
      docs/research/unit-501redbranchpin-2026-11-22/plant-a.mjs

  A brace scan that starts at the first `{` is wrong on four live mechanisms in this tree: a TS type literal or an object default in the parameter list, a JSDoc `@type` brace, and a brace-bearing return type. Clear this by, in preference order:
    1. import { bodyOf, declBodies } from '<…>/scripts/lib/decl-body.mjs';
    2. put `brace-slicer-deliberate: <reason>` at the site, if the file's job is to reproduce the defect (a comparison arm, a mutant, a fixture);
    3. append the path to scripts/decl-body-slicers.baseline, which states that a known unguarded slicer is being added on purpose.
```

## 4. What each stream carries, keyed on content rather than on bytes

  tracked files read   stdout: 1052   stderr: ABSENT
  population           stdout: 66   stderr: ABSENT
  MARKED               stdout: 4   stderr: ABSENT
  UNGUARDED            stdout: 59   stderr: ABSENT
  baseline entries     stdout: 58   stderr: ABSENT

  plant path          stdout: ABSENT   stderr: PRESENT
  announced count     stdout: ABSENT   stderr: PRESENT

Every tree-keyed figure is on stdout and nowhere else. Every defect-keyed fact is on stderr and
nowhere else. The two streams are not two views of one report; they are a tree report and a
defect report, and a byte pin cannot mean the same thing on both.
